Privacy score and site trust profiles (FEATURES 3.2 / 3.4; Phase 4)

Closes the two "later tier" Phase 4 items and the per-site drilldown
follow-up, which turned out to be the same feature seen from two directions.

PRIVACY SCORE (3.2). The Active Protections list already knew which
protections were on; it just never added them up or offered to fix any of
them. It now reads from one table that drives all three surfaces -- the
status rows, the score, and a "to improve" list where each entry explains in
plain language what the protection does and offers a button that turns it on.
Previously the rows were a hardcoded array; three lists of the same facts
would have drifted the first time a protection was added.

Two protections were missing from the posture list entirely and are now
scored: encrypted DNS (network.trr.mode) and delete-cookies-on-close. Both are
things a privacy browser should have an opinion about, and neither was
visible anywhere in the pane.

Every check counts the same. Weighting them would mean asserting that, say,
fingerprinting protection is worth 1.4 cookie protections -- a number nobody
could defend, quietly encoding an opinion as arithmetic. Equal weights are
honest about what the score is: a count of protections that are on, shown as
a percentage. trr.mode 2 and 3 both count as on; 3 is encrypted-only, which
is a reliability choice rather than a stronger privacy one, and failing closed
on DNS is not a default to score people down for declining.

A check with no automatic fix is still scored and still explained, but no
button is offered. A control that looks actionable and does nothing is worse
than no control.

SITE TRUST PROFILES (3.4) and the per-site drilldown. FEATURES describes 3.4
as "youtube.com: video, login, tracking, third-party cookies" -- a per-site
card rather than a per-capability list. That is exactly what the drilldown
follow-up asked for too, so it ships once: a picker over every origin with
stored state, and a card showing that site's permissions and cookie rule
together, each individually removable, plus Forget this site.

The permission manager and this pane are the same data pivoted. Per-capability
answers "who can use my microphone"; per-site answers "what has this site
talked me into". Both questions get asked, and neither view is derived from a
separate store, so they cannot disagree.

The selected site survives re-render. perm-changed fires on every removal, and
a picker that jumped back to the first site each time would be unusable for
the one job this pane exists to do -- cleaning up after a site.

Verified without a build:
  * node --check on the pane script; the fragment parses as well-formed XML
    under the XUL + XHTML namespaces.
  * All 87 Fluent ids the Privacy Center now references resolve against
    zen-preferences.ftl, with no dead strings left behind.

NOT DONE, and not claimed: the blocked-today BADGE surface. The count itself
is in the pane and always has been; the follow-up asks for it on a chrome
surface -- the toolbar or the menu button -- which means touching
KavachaMenu and the chrome markup rather than the settings pane. That is a
different patch against different files, and it is left open in
REMAINING_WORK rather than quietly folded into this one.

Also not verified: the fix buttons against a live profile, and the card
against a real permission store. Both are build-and-click arms.
